    "sourceText": "after CSSS26, Definition 2.1, pp. 141–142.",
    "sourceHtml": "after <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jalgebra.2026.04.058\">CSSS26</a>, Definition 2.1, pp. 141–142.",
    "leftHtml": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> be a quiver and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi></math> an ideal of <span class=\"math-run\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>𝕜</mi><mi>Q</mi></mrow></math>.</span> The pair <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is a gentle presentation if:</p>\n<ol><li>Each vertex is the source of at most two arrows and the target of at most two arrows.</li><li>For each arrow <span class=\"math-run\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi></math>,</span> at most one arrow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>b</mi></math> has <span class=\"math-run\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>,</span> and at most one arrow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>c</mi></math> has <span class=\"math-run\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>.</span></li><li>For each arrow <span class=\"math-run\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi></math>,</span> at most one arrow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>b</mi></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <span class=\"math-run\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>,</span> and at most one arrow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>c</mi></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <span class=\"math-run\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>.</span></li><li>The ideal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi></math> is generated by paths of length <span class=\"math-run\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>.</span></li></ol>\n<p>It is finite dimensional gentle if, in addition, there is an integer <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> such that every path of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>N</mi></math> lies in <span class=\"math-run\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi></math>.</span> An algebra is gentle, respectively finite dimensional gentle, if it is Morita equivalent to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>𝕜</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace=\"0\" rspace=\"0\">/</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> for such a presentation.</p>",
    "rightHtml": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> be a quiver and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi></math> an ideal of <span class=\"math-run\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>𝕜</mi><mi>Q</mi></mrow></math>,</span> with products in travel order. The pair <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is a gentle presentation if:</p>\n<ol><li>Each vertex is the source of at most two arrows and the target of at most two arrows.</li><li>For each arrow <span class=\"math-run\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi></math>,</span> at most one arrow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>b</mi></math> has <span class=\"math-run\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>,</span> and at most one arrow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>c</mi></math> has <span class=\"math-run\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>.</span></li><li>For each arrow <span class=\"math-run\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi></math>,</span> at most one arrow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>b</mi></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <span class=\"math-run\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>,</span> and at most one arrow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>c</mi></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <span class=\"math-run\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>.</span></li><li>The ideal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi></math> is generated by paths of length <span class=\"math-run\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>.</span></li></ol>\n<p>It is finite dimensional gentle if, in addition, there is an integer <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> such that every path of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>N</mi></math> lies in <span class=\"math-run\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi></math>.</span> An algebra is gentle, respectively finite dimensional gentle, if it is Morita equivalent to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>𝕜</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace=\"0\" rspace=\"0\">/</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> for such a presentation.</p>",
    "applicationHtml": "The input is a finite quiver with specified quadratic monomial zero relations. Validation checks the gentle conditions and rejects an oriented cycle of allowed successors, which would make the algebra infinite dimensional."
